module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.realization_arms_diverge_on_whether_the_program_refuses

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data realization_arms_diverge_on_whether_the_program_refuses: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "realization_arms_diverge_on_whether_the_program_refuses" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "realization arms diverge on WHETHER THE PROGRAM REFUSES (two realizations of one accepted program agree on the returned value and disagree on whether an evaluation-time refusal fires, because the fact that decides it -- evaluation order -- is modeled nowhere and each arm improvises).",

    "INVALID STATE: a connective whose operand evaluation strategy is unmodeled, realized strictly by one arm and lazily by another.",

    "gunbc v1 today: v1.compiler.interpreter eval_expr_inner's ExprBinOp arm evaluates BOTH operands through `?` before dispatching.",

    "eval_binop DOES carry And and Or arms -- `Value::Bool(left.is_truthy() && right.is_truthy())` -- and they are irrelevant to this class and worth naming precisely because they look like the repair: they run on operands ALREADY EVALUATED at the call site, so their Rust && is over two bools and short-circuits nothing.",

    "A reader grepping eval_binop for And finds a hit and concludes the case is handled, while std.operator_realization maps BinOp::And and BinOp::Or to OperatorRealization::HostOperator, rendered as Rust && and ||, which short-circuit.",

    "HARM: the guard idiom -- a cheap precondition guarding an expression that can refuse -- means two different programs.",

    "The interpreter runs the guarded expression when the guard is FALSE and refuses; emitted Rust never runs it and returns a value.",

    "Silent in both directions and invisible to any oracle comparing returned values on guard-true inputs, which is every value-comparison oracle we have.",

    "DISCRIMINATING RED, executed 2026-09-02, not asserted: `fn guarded_division(n: Int) -> Bool { n != 0 && (100 / n) > 1 }`.",

    "At n=0 the interpreter refuses DivisionByZero and the emitted Rust returns false; at n=5 both return true.",

    "Division by zero was chosen deliberately because BOTH realizations abort identically IF the expression is evaluated, so evaluation order is the only variable in the pair.",

    "CONTROLS, because a false from a harness that cannot observe an abort is a vacuous green: the same function with the guard forced true and n=0 PANICKED with 'attempt to divide by zero' and was caught and reported, so the harness can see the abort; and the guard-true positive control agrees across both arms.",

    "HONESTY BOUND ON THE EVIDENCE: the interpreter arm ran on the real acceptance path via `gunbc run`; the emitted arm executed the emitted function bodies extracted VERBATIM into a rustc harness, not the whole emitted crate.",

    "WHY IT IS NOT refusal_deferred_to_emitted_runtime: that class is a compiler writing a refusal construct INTO the artifact while reporting success.",

    "Here no refusal is written anywhere and the compile is honest; the two arms simply disagree at run time about whether one fires.",

    "Same neighbourhood, different invalid state, different repair.",

    "THE POPULATION IS SURVIVORSHIP-FILTERED AND A GREP UNDERSTATES IT BY CONSTRUCTION.",

    "The interpreter is the STRICTER arm, so any author who wrote the guard idiom over a refusing right operand hit the refusal while authoring and rewrote it -- most likely into a nested if or match.",

    "So the sites matching `cheap_guard && expensive` are the SURVIVORS, the cases where the right operand happens not to refuse; the cases carrying the correctness consequence were already edited away.",

    "A LOW COUNT IS THEREFORE NOT EVIDENCE THE CLASS IS MINOR AND MUST NOT BE REPORTED AS ONE.",

    "The honest population is sites where an author WANTED the guard idiom, which lives in the rewrites and is not reachable by the same needle.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE, stated to generalise past this operator: wherever a construct is realized independently by the interpreter and by an emission target, ask what fact decides its behaviour and where that fact is DECLARED.",

    "If the deciding fact is absent from the authority both arms are supposed to consult, the arms are not implementing one semantics, they are each inventing one, and agreement on the values you happened to test is not evidence they agree.",

    "The tell is a realization shape -- HostOperator -- that names WHO evaluates without naming WHAT is evaluated.",

    "RUNG: mitigatable at best, and only because one arm refuses loudly; against a source-to-emission path this is silent wrongness, which DESIGN section 4b places outside the ladder.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, named as a capability and not an artifact: EVALUATION ORDER MODELED AS A PROPERTY OF A CONNECTIVE IN THE .dag AUTHORITY AND CONSULTED BY BOTH REALIZATIONS -- sufficient for the interpreter's binop arm to derive its strictness from the same row the emitter derives its rendering from, so that a connective's operand strategy cannot be stated twice or left unstated.",

    "A grep across dag/, src/v2/ and src/v1/*.dag on 2026-09-02 found no such fact: every short-circuit hit is prose in a comment or a witness name.",

    "NOT REPAIRED HERE: changing the interpreter to short-circuit is a corpus-wide evaluation-semantics change and needs its own subject, population and review; filing it against the missing carrier is the point of this row.",

    "REPAIRED 2026-09-21 UNDER ITS OWN SUBJECT, which is what the sentence above asked for. The occasion was the EFFECT form of the same divergence, found by a peer lane: 'false && Filesystem.Write(...)' WROTE the file under the interpreter, so 'created && !chmod(base)' chmodded a squatted directory -- code that reads as guarded and executed unconditionally.",

    "THE CARRIER THIS ROW'S TRIGGER NAMED NOW EXISTS: std.operator_realization OperandDemand and operand_demand, a total fold over the BinOp vocabulary answering WHICH OPERANDS AN OPERATOR ASKS FOR. DemandsBothOperands for the arithmetic and relational operators; DemandsRightOnlyWhenLeftIs { deciding } for And (true) and Or (false); DemandsRightOnlyWhenLeftIsAbsent for NullCoalesce.",

    "THE VALUE WAS DERIVED, NOT CHOSEN, AND THAT IS WHY THE REPAIR IS A REPAIR RATHER THAN A SECOND SEMANTICS: std.logic classical_and is 'match a { False => False  True => b }' and classical_or is its dual, so the corpus's own logic authority already demanded the right operand under exactly one left value. The interpreter was the arm disagreeing with the declared authority; the emitted Rust already agreed with it.",

    "BOTH REALIZATIONS NOW READ THAT ONE ROW, which is the whole content of the trigger and the reason it is not discharged by either arm alone. v1.compiler.interpreter eval_expr_inner's ExprBinOp arm evaluates the LEFT operand, consults operand_demand, and evaluates the right only when the row demands it. v1.compiler.emit_rust emit_rust_demanded_host_bin_op compares the declared demand against what the Rust token for that operator asks for and derives the rendering from the comparison, so the emitter's laziness is no longer a property of the glyph table.",

    "THE EMITTER'S THREE OUTCOMES ARE ALL REACHABLE BY EDITING THE ROW RATHER THAN THE SOURCE, which is what keeps the fold a derivation instead of a decoration (DESIGN section 4b, ask whether the RED is authorable): agreement splices the host token, so today's emitted bytes do not move; an operator declaring DemandsBothOperands whose token would skip the right operand binds both to temporaries first; and an operator declaring a conditional right operand whose token evaluates both REFUSES, because no infix form carries it and guessing one is silent wrongness.",

    "NULL-COALESCING WAS CARRIED RATHER THAN OMITTED, because it had the identical divergence and declaring the row while leaving one arm contradicting it would be worse than not declaring it: the interpreter evaluated both operands before selecting, while extdeps.languages.rust.emit rust_null_coalesce_template renders '{0}.unwrap_or_else(|| {1})', whose closure body is demanded only when the left is absent.",

    "THE CENSUS WAS RESOLVED AT INTENT BEFORE THE FLIP, NOT SILENCED BY IT, because a site that quietly stops working is the same class one layer along. Four sites put a real effect on the right of a connective and each wanted the effect to run unconditionally, so each now binds it: gunbc.instruments.github_app_acquire custody_probe_cleanup (three of its four shell.Remove.RecursiveForce calls would have stopped running, leaving a file named like a private key behind, which the annotation above that function calls worse than no receipt); test.claim.spark.pair_serving_authority_log_real_execution (a kill -9 sequenced between two settles, whose skipping would leak the spawned process onto the host); test.manual.runner_microvm_lifecycle_wet_receipt stop_incarnation_unit, whose guard was CONTROL FLOW and is now an if; and gunbc.bmc.bmc_fan_program_observation_witness, two independent reads now bound separately. Two further sites put a pure shell.Test.IsFile on the right and are dispositioned unchanged: the operator's value is identical under both demands and the call has no effect.",

    "THE THREE ENROLLED WET PROBES IN test.claim.eval_model_probe FLIPPED DELIBERATELY AND THEIR POLARITY WAS RE-DERIVED FROM THE NEW MEASUREMENT, never edited to stay green: they asserted the marker was GONE after a removal under a false left, and they now assert it SURVIVES. a_let_bound_effect_still_runs was enrolled beside them as the control that the same removal on the same path does run when it is demanded, without which a surviving marker is indistinguishable from a broken probe.",

    "THIS ROW'S OWN RED IS NOW ENROLLED RATHER THAN RECORDED: guarded_division in test.claim.eval_model_probe carries 'n != 0 && (100 / n) > 1' with the n = 0 and n = 5 claims beside it. It is the reason the repair declares the demand instead of refusing effects under operands -- ITS EFFECT ROW IS EMPTY, so an effect-shaped wall would have left it diverging while being cited as having closed this class.",

    "RUNG AFTER THE REPAIR, STATED AT THE GRAIN THE EVIDENCE SUPPORTS AND NOT AT THE CLASS'S FULL BREADTH. For the BinOp vocabulary the invalid state is no longer writable: operand_demand is a TOTAL fold over a closed coproduct, so an operator with no declared strategy fails to compile, and neither realization carries a strategy of its own to disagree with it. The earlier RUNG line above stands unedited as the reading at the time it was written; this is its successor, not its correction.",

    "WHAT DID NOT CLIMB, named so the row is not read as retired: the RECOGNITION RULE above generalises past this operator to ANY construct realized independently by the interpreter and by an emission target, and that population is not enumerated anywhere. Its next-rung trigger is unchanged in kind -- for each such construct, the fact deciding its behaviour declared in the .dag authority both arms consult -- and BinOp is the first member to reach it, not the last.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
